---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import data from '../data/tribe_conversion.json';

// Mechanic transcribed from Tile.doTribeTurn (Tile.cs ~11372) and
// Game.findNearestSettlementTribe (Game.cs:15111). Numbers from tribeLevel.xml
// via build_tribe_conversion.py.
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const { levels, search, adopters, barbarian, counts } = data as any;
const converting = levels.filter((l: any) => l.canConvert);
const milestones = converting[0]?.odds.map((o: any) => o.turnsAfter) ?? [];
---

<Base
  title="Barbarian Site Conversion"
  active="tribe-conversion"
  pageMark="🏕️"
  description="How barbarian sites become tribal sites in Old World. From a set turn onward, each barbarian settlement rolls a few percent per turn to be adopted by a tribe that has a settlement within 12 tiles, and the roll only succeeds when no player unit and no damaged barbarian is nearby. Includes start turns and per-turn odds by tribe level."
  pageStats={[
    { label: 'levels that convert', value: `${counts.convertingLevels} of ${levels.length}` },
    { label: 'to nearest settlement', value: `${search.range} tiles` },
    { label: 'adopting tribes', value: counts.adopters },
  ]}
>
  <p class="lede">
    Late in a game, a barbarian camp can be <b>adopted by a nearby tribe</b>, which hands the
    site and the barbarians on it to that tribe. This page shows when that can happen, how
    likely it is, and how to stop it.
  </p>

  <section class="tc">
    <h2 class="tc__h">The roll, step by step</h2>
    <ol class="tc__steps">
      <li><b>Only barbarian sites are eligible:</b> {barbarian.map((b: any) => b.name).join(', ')}.
        A site already held by a real tribe never rolls again.</li>
      <li><b>The clock has to have started.</b> Nothing happens until the game passes the tribe
        level's start turn (below).</li>
      <li><b>Roll the percent</b>, once per site per turn.</li>
      <li><b>Find an adopter.</b> A tribe qualifies if it owns a settlement within
        <b>{search.range} tiles</b> of the site in a straight line, across mountains and water.
        Among those, the site goes to the tribe whose settlement is closest by an actual path over
        land or water. With no qualifying tribe there is no conversion.</li>
      <li><b>The site must be quiet.</b> The conversion fails that turn if <em>any</em> player
        unit is within <b>{converting[0]?.unitRange ?? 3}</b> tiles of the site, or any of the
        site's barbarians is <em>damaged</em>.</li>
      <li><b>The handover.</b> The site and each barbarian in range become the new tribe's. A
        barbarian that can strike a city from where it stands becomes a <b>raider</b> against
        that city instead.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2 class="tc__h">When it can happen, and how likely</h2>
    <p class="tc__intro">
      Both the start turn and the chance per turn depend on the <b>Tribe Level</b> setting. The
      later columns give the odds that one undisturbed site has converted after that many
      eligible turns.
    </p>
    <div class="grid"><div class="grid__scroll">
      <table class="ntbl tc__tbl">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th class="rowlabel rowlabel--corner">Tribe level</th>
            <th class="nhdr nhdr--num">Starts after turn</th>
            <th class="nhdr nhdr--num">Chance / turn</th>
            {milestones.map((n: number) => (
              <th class="nhdr nhdr--num">after {n} turns</th>
            ))}
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {levels.map((l: any) => (
            <tr class={l.canConvert ? '' : 'tc__off'}>
              <td class="rowlabel tc__name">{l.name}</td>
              {l.canConvert ? (
                <>
                  <td class="cell cell--num">{l.startTurn}</td>
                  <td class="cell cell--num tc__pct">{l.percent}%</td>
                  {l.odds.map((o: any) => (
                    <td class="cell cell--num">{o.chance}%<span class="tc__sub">by turn {o.byTurn}</span></td>
                  ))}
                </>
              ) : (
                <td class="cell cell--num tc__never" colspan={2 + milestones.length}>never converts</td>
              )}
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div></div>
    <p class="tc__note">
      Higher tribe levels convert <b>earlier and faster</b>: Raging starts 30 turns before Weak
      and rolls twice as often. On <b>Passive</b>, and with tribes off, sites never convert.
    </p>

    <h2 class="tc__h">Who can adopt a site</h2>
    <p class="tc__intro">
      Only the {counts.adopters} tribes you can negotiate with can adopt a site, and only from a
      settlement of their own within range.
    </p>
    <div class="tc__tribes">
      {adopters.map((t: any) => (
        <span class="tc__tribe">{t.name}</span>
      ))}
    </div>
    <h2 class="tc__h">Why the adopter can look far away</h2>
    <p class="tc__intro">
      The {search.range} tiles are measured to <b>the tribe's nearest settlement</b>, not to
      wherever you think that tribe lives. That explains conversions that look impossible.
    </p>
    <ul class="tc__why">
      <li><b>Tribes own scattered settlements.</b> A tribe's main cluster can sit 30 tiles away
        while one outlying camp of theirs sits 9 tiles from the site being converted. You see the
        name and colour of a distant tribe, and the game measured to the near camp.</li>
      <li><b>Conversions chain.</b> An adopted site counts as one of the new tribe's
        settlements, so it can bring the <em>next</em> barbarian camp {search.range} tiles
        further out into range. A tribe can cross the map one adoption at a time.</li>
    </ul>

    <p class="tc__note">
      A barbarian camp near an established tribe will probably convert sooner or later, and then
      you have a neighbour you can trade, truce and ally with instead of a permanent enemy. To
      block it, park a unit next to the camp or leave one of its defenders wounded.
    </p>

    <p class="tc__see">
      See also <a href={`${base}tribes`}>Tribes</a> and
      <a href={`${base}camp-spawning`}>Camp Unit Spawning</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .lede { max-width: 88ch; color: var(--text-dim); font-size: 0.86rem; line-height: 1.6; margin: 0 0 1.4rem; }
  .lede b { color: var(--text); }

  .tc__h { font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: 1.1rem; color: var(--accent); margin: 1.6rem 0 0.5rem; }
  .tc__h:first-of-type { margin-top: 0; }
  .tc__intro, .tc__note { max-width: 88ch; font-size: 0.82rem; color: var(--text-dim); line-height: 1.6; margin: 0 0 0.8rem; }
  .tc__note { color: var(--text-faint); margin-top: 0.7rem; }
  .tc__intro b, .tc__note b { color: var(--text); }

  .tc__steps { max-width: 88ch; margin: 0 0 0.6rem; padding-left: 1.2rem; display: grid; gap: 0.45rem;
    font-size: 0.84rem; color: var(--text-dim); line-height: 1.55; }
  .tc__steps b { color: var(--text); }

  .tc__tbl { max-width: 62rem; }
  .tc__name { text-align: left; font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: 0.9rem; color: var(--text); }
  .nhdr--num, .tc__tbl :global(.cell--num) { text-align: right; }
  .tc__tbl :global(.cell--num) { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
    font-size: 0.84rem; vertical-align: top; }
  .tc__pct { color: var(--gold); }
  .tc__sub { display: block; font-family: var(--font-body); font-size: 0.58rem; color: var(--text-faint); }
  .tc__never { color: var(--text-faint); font-style: italic; text-align: left !important; }
  .tc__off .tc__name { color: var(--text-faint); }

  .tc__why { max-width: 88ch; margin: 0 0 0.4rem; padding-left: 1.1rem; display: grid; gap: 0.45rem;
    font-size: 0.82rem; color: var(--text-dim); line-height: 1.55; }
  .tc__why b { color: var(--text); }
  .tc__tribes { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 0.35rem; margin: 0 0 0.4rem; }
  .tc__tribe { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.72rem; color: var(--text-soft);
    background: var(--bg-elev); border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 4px; padding: 0.15rem 0.5rem; }

  .tc__see { margin-top: 1.4rem; font-size: 0.82rem; color: var(--text-dim); }
  .tc__see a { color: var(--accent); }
</style>
